## DESCRIPTION
## Computations of numbers with units and specified significant figures.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(WeBWorK)
## DBchapter(WeBWorK tutorial)
## DBsection(PGML tutorial 2015)
## Date(08/01/2026)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## MO(1)
## KEYWORDS('significant figures')

#:% name = Significant Figures with Units
#:% type = [technique, sample]
#:% category = [misc]
#:% see_also = [SignificantFigures.pg, SignificantFiguresFlags.pg]

#:% section = preamble
#: The macros `contextSignificantFigures.pl` and `contextUnits.pl` need to
#: be added to the `loadMacros`.
DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',                'PGML.pl',
    'contextSignificantFigures.pl', 'contextUnits.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

#:% section = setup
#:
#: Since units and Significant Figures are both to be used, start with the
#: `Context()` function at the top.  For this example, volume units are to be
#: used, hence the argument for the `withUnitsFor` method.  See
#: PODLINK('contextUnits.pl') for details on all available unit categories.
#:
#: Since random numbers are used in this, first the number is calculated using
#: `random` and `Real`.  On the construction of the number with `Real`, the
#: option `sigfigs` is used to specify 4 significant figures.  This is to
#: ensure we have 4 significant figures even if the random number chosen was
#: 135 or 150 for example.
#:
#: The units are included with the `Compute` function.   For the sum, any
#: valid answer in any unit is accepted, such as in liters or cups. 
#: However, if the answer is desired in a particular unit, add
#: `->cmp(sameUnits => 1)` to the answer.  Note, if it is desired to ask a
#: question about the volume in a different unit, say quarts, define
#: `$c2 = $c->toUnits('qt');` and then for the answer, call
#: `$c2->cmp(sameUnits => 1)`.

Context(context::Units::extending("SignificantFigures")->withUnitsFor('volume')
);

$a1 = Real(random(100, 200, 0.1),  sigfigs => 4);
$b1 = Real(random(50,  90,  0.01), sigfigs => 4);

$a = Compute("$a1 ml");
$b = Compute("$b1 ml");
$c = $a + $b;

#:% section = statement
BEGIN_PGML
A lab technician has a beaker with [$a] of water.  She adds [$b] to the beaker.  
Using the proper number of significant figures, what is the total 
amount in the beaker? 

total volume: [_]{$c}
END_PGML

#:% section = solution
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Solution explanation goes here.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
